    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "As part of an integrated structural and diagenetic study on fault sealing, small faults in sandstone, collected from core and outcrop, were studied using SEM cathodoluminescence and cold-cathode luminescence. SEM cathodoluminescence revealed small amounts of quartz cement in fault rock not visible in conventional transmitted light microscopy. These small amounts of quartz cement may be significant in occluding pore throats and reducing cross-fault permeability. Cold-cathode luminescence provided color images suitable for textural analyses of fault rock. Based on cold-cathode luminescence petrography, certain clay and mica bearing sand- stones were found to contain foliated cataclasite formed by fragment tails originating at larger broken grains. It is inferred that small amounts of clay in sandstone may inhibit mixing of grain fragments during slip and cataclasis and thus contribute to a textural and possible permeability anisotropy in fault rock.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
